    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kangley sprang in 1881 as a company town built by the Star Coal Company to house miners working nearby bituminous seams.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kangley_illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village sits atop a bluff west of the Vermilion River, which drains northwest toward the Illinois River at LaSalle.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kangley%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Just three miles northwest of Streator, Kangley lies along Illinois Route 71, keeping close ties to the regional rail corridor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kangley%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "John Kangley, born in County Meath, Ireland, arrived in Illinois at age eleven and later founded the town that bears his name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kangley_illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Star Coal Company’s Kangley & Carbon Hill Mine operated from the 1880s until the early 1900s, marking the town’s mining heyday.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
